[Unit]
Description=Synthetic Enterprise worker tick — one scheduled bounded-invocation check (SCHEDULED_BOUNDED_INVOCATIONS_DESIGN.md, 2026-07-20). Cheap Python: sync origin-staged docs + find_work; spawn ONE `claude -p` bounded worker invocation ONLY if there is work; at rest it exits at ~0 tokens (no model). Replaces the persistent stop-hook-rearm seat + rest-heartbeat (input-blocking / death-on-API-error / token-burn). Triggered by worker-tick.timer (60s backstop) AND worker-tick.path (immediate on a staged doc).
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/home/rich/synthetic-enterprise
# NTFY secret via the Option-2 out-of-tree location; `-` = still run (and log) if absent.
EnvironmentFile=-/home/rich/.config/synthetic-enterprise/.env.ntfy
# The tick BLOCKS until the bounded invocation it spawns exits (Type=oneshot must not return early,
# or systemd tears down the cgroup and kills the child). TimeoutStartSec bounds a hung invocation:
# systemd SIGTERMs the whole cgroup (tick + claude -p), so a hang becomes a skipped tick
# (the next trigger starts fresh), never a wedge. This is the bounded-invocation guarantee in IaC.
#
# ── 1800 WAS BELOW HONEST TICK DURATION, AND THE MEASUREMENT WAS CENSORED BY THE BOUND ITSELF ──
# (2026-08-10, director-flagged: "three ticks were killed by TimeoutStartSec mid-work".) Over the
# three days to 2026-08-10 this unit was timeout-killed 28 times. The wall-clock distribution from
# its own journal:  n=31, min 5.0 min, median 30.0, p90 30.0, max 30.0, 29 of 31 over 25 min.
#
# Median == p90 == max == exactly the bound is not a distribution, it is a RIGHT-CENSORED one: the
# control being measured is what truncates the measurement, so the honest duration of a real tick is
# NOT DERIVABLE from this data. All that can be said is that at least 28 invocations wanted more
# than 30 minutes. Choosing "a bit above the observed max" would therefore be choosing a bit above
# the bound that caused the problem -- the number would be an artefact of the defect
# (cf. feedback_never_pin_generated_values_in_controls, and the as_of-population artefact class).
#
# So this is deliberately set FAR above any plausible honest tick (4x the old bound) for one purpose:
# to UNCENSOR the measurement. Once ticks are free to run to their natural length, the distribution
# becomes observable and the bound can be re-derived from it at a real percentile. Atom
# OPS_rederive_tick_timeout carries that follow-up; until it lands this number is a deliberate
# over-estimate, not a calibrated one, and it is documented as such rather than presented as fitted.
#
# The kill was never costless: it tears down the WHOLE cgroup, including Agent forks whose work was
# still uncommitted (see FORK SALVAGE below) -- so an undersized bound destroyed BUILT WORK, which
# is why erring long is the safe direction here and erring short is not.
TimeoutStartSec=7200
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 -m background.worker_tick

# ── FORK SALVAGE (director console, 2026-08-03: "mechanise the merge-or-reap so this can't recur") ──
# CORRECTION to the comment above: the 30-min SIGTERM was NOT costless. Because systemd tears down
# the WHOLE cgroup, the kill also killed every Agent fork the invocation had dispatched — and their
# work was still UNCOMMITTED in their worktrees. On 2026-08-03 this unit was timeout-killed 10 times
# (~37% of the invocations carrying real work), which is why the repo accumulated 8 branches of
# hand-written "RESCUE: preserve this dead fork's uncommitted build" commits and a 10:37 audit
# recording 2,566 lines stranded in dying worktrees. A killed tick cost BUILT WORK, not just time.
#
# The doorbell's "each fork MUST come home" was only ever PROSE in a prompt (supervisor.py:3492),
# executed LAST, inside a process under a hard wall-clock kill — so the step that would have saved
# the work was precisely the step the axe fell on. MAKE_IT_STICK predicted this: an exhortation
# decays, a mechanism holds. THIS is the mechanism.
#
# ExecStopPost runs AFTER the main process is gone, on EVERY exit path — clean exit, crash, and the
# SIGTERM timeout alike — so it fires exactly where the loss happened. It commits each dirty fork
# worktree to THAT FORK'S OWN branch: never merges to main, never deletes, never touches the shared
# main tree. After it runs, "built but uncommitted" is not a reachable state, so a kill costs
# wall-clock and never built work. `-` prefix: a failing salvage must not fail the unit.
TimeoutStopSec=120
ExecStopPost=-/usr/bin/python3 -m background.fork_salvage
